# Traefik's domain-fronting protection works by comparing two TLS option
# names per request: the SNICheck middleware (pkg/middlewares/snicheck)
# returns 421 when the option actually used for the connection (resolved
# from the SNI in the ClientHello) differs from the option the matched
# router's Host rule resolves to. Those router-side names are computed by
# resolveHTTPTLSOptions in pkg/server/aggregator.go, which walks every
# HTTP router's rule domains and writes router.TLS.ResolvedOptions. The
# defect: applyConfigurations in pkg/server/configurationwatcher.go ran
# that resolution inside mergeConfiguration, i.e. BEFORE applyModel
# expands each router into per-entrypoint copies and gives routers with
# no router-level TLS section the entrypoint model's TLS block. For a
# router whose mTLS-bearing options arrive only through the entrypoint
# model (RequireAndVerifyClientCert declared at the entrypoint or under a
# wildcard Host rule), the resolved option is therefore computed against
# absent TLS state, and the SNI-vs-Host comparison that fronts mTLS
# enforcement happens against the wrong name -- a client could handshake
# under a permissive SNI on the same entrypoint and reach the protected
# host with no client certificate (CWE-288; the affected symbol is
# SNICheck.ServeHTTP). The fixed revision, a664812e9c30f8c9705ce16f0d2f2d96e017bccd,
# is two moved lines: mergeConfiguration returns conf untouched and the
# watcher calls resolveHTTPTLSOptions after applyModel instead. The
# middleware itself is byte-identical across the fixed revision.
#
# Pinned as a miss: nothing reports either revision. Measured over three
# --cache off runs of pkg/server per revision: 32 findings each time, same
# rule histogram (8 CFG-006, 7 RES-001, 6 CFG-004, 3 INJ-010, 3 CRY-003,
# 2 CFG-001, LIFE-001, CRY-001, CFG-014), zero findings on aggregator.go,
# configurationwatcher.go, snicheck.go or tcp/manager.go. vyql match on
# the snicheck package labels only the request parameter as HttpInput
# (source, no sink, no control), and on configurationwatcher.go and
# aggregator.go labels nothing at all.
#
# This is the measured family "authorization decided by wiring or
# configuration, not dataflow" (ranks 1514, 1535, 1540, 1556, 1557,
# 1593): the enforcement code is present and correct in both revisions
# and the fixed revision reorders two calls in a configuration pipeline, so no taint
# path, guard text or sink differs. The 1380 counterweight does not
# rescue it: the call the fixed revision leaves inside the guarded method is
# resolveHTTPTLSOptions, a package-private Traefik internal with no
# documentation, not an API-documented library call. The exhaustive
# enumeration of the shape's reachable set: across the whole tree
# resolveHTTPTLSOptions and applyModel each appear at exactly two non-test
# sites (definition plus the single call the fixed revision moves), and across every
# sibling clone under /tmp/cve-work/rank*/ the name exists nowhere outside
# this repository -- a binding keyed on it fires on one function in one
# codebase, dies under the rename test, and is the fixing commit's own
# layout as discriminator (the shape AGENT-CONTEXT records as rejected).
# The nearest adjacent shipped rule, VYQL-AUTH-077
# (code.PostTlsIdentityRebindingMissingCheck), covers the missing-check
# form of domain fronting; here the check exists and is fed mis-ordered
# inputs, so it is rejected on both snippets deliberately. The
# attribution-gap family (1377/1389/1399) does not apply: no check left
# the function -- the check never changed.

test "rank1867: tls options resolved before entrypoint models expand the routers"
  lang go
  file pkg/server/configurationwatcher.go
  reject VYQL-AUTH-077
  code
  ```
  package server

  import (
  	"context"
  	"reflect"

  	"github.com/traefik/traefik/v2/pkg/config/dynamic"
  )

  func mergeConfiguration(configurations dynamic.Configurations, defaultEntryPointName string) dynamic.Configuration {
  	conf := dynamic.Merge(configurations)
  	return resolveHTTPTLSOptions(conf)
  }

  func (c *ConfigurationWatcher) applyConfigurations(ctx context.Context) {
  	var lastConfigurations dynamic.Configurations
  	for {
  		select {
  		case <-ctx.Done():
  			return
  		case newConfigs, ok := <-c.newConfigs:
  			if !ok {
  				return
  			}
  			conf := mergeConfiguration(newConfigs.DeepCopy(), c.defaultEntryPoints)
  			conf = applyModel(conf)

  			for _, listener := range c.configurationListeners {
  				listener(conf)
  			}
  			lastConfigurations = newConfigs
  		}
  	}
  }
  ```

